<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Protest and freedom · 1905 · South &amp; Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Ekla Chalo Re</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="bn">Jôdi Tor Dak Shune Keu Na Ase Tôbe Ekla Chôlo Re</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A song Rabindranath Tagore wrote in 1905 telling people to keep going on their own road even if nobody joins them.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Rabindranath Tagore</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Rabindranath Tagore</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the Swadeshi movement in Bengal; Bengali song (Rabindra Sangeet)</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bengali</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Giridih, present-day Jharkhand, India</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1905; first published September 1905</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Protest and freedom</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words and music by Rabindranath Tagore (published 1905))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabindranath-tagore">Rabindranath Tagore</a> wrote &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ekla-chalo-re">Ekla Chalo Re</a>&quot; in 1905 at Giridih, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jharkhand">Jharkhand</a>. It was one of 22 protest songs he wrote in the Swadeshi period of the Indian freedom movement, and with &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amar-sonar-bangla">Amar Sonar Bangla</a>&quot; it became one of the key songs of the movement against the partition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> Presidency in 1905. It was based on and influenced by &quot;Harinaam Diye Jagat Matale Amar Ekla Nitai Re&quot;, a popular Bengali Vaishnavite kirtan of the Dhapkirtan or Manoharshahi gharana in praise of Nityananda, disciple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaitanya-mahaprabhu">Chaitanya Mahaprabhu</a>.</p><p>Titled &quot;Eka&quot; (&quot;Alone&quot;), it was first published in the September 1905 issue of the magazine Bhandar and included the same year in Tagore's song anthology Baul. In 1941 it was placed in the &quot;Swadesh&quot; (&quot;Homeland&quot;) section of Gitabitan, the complete anthology of Tagore's songs. Its musical notation was prepared by Indira Devi, Tagore's niece, first published in the April–May 1906 issue of Sangeet-Vignan Prakashika and later included in the 46th volume of Swarabitan, the complete collection of Tagore's notations.</p><p>Tagore himself first recorded the song sometime between 1905 and 1908, on a cylinder labelled H. Bose Swadeshi Records that is now lost. Two other early records were made by Harendranath Dutta, for the Gramophone Company of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindustan">Hindustan</a> Party (Amala Dutta, Nandita Devi, Sudhin Dutta and Santidev Ghosh), for Hindustan Records. The Rabindra Sangeet singer Suchitra <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitra">Mitra</a> recorded it four times: in 1948 for the film Sandipan Pathshala, in 1984, on the album Rupantori (1988), and with the sarod of Ustad Amjad Ali Khan on Tribute to Tagore.</p><p>The song urges the listener to go on with their journey even when others abandon them or give no support. It was a favourite of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a> and is often quoted in movements for social and political change. Its tune was used with Hindi words in the 2004 film <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subhas-chandra-bose">Netaji</a> Subhas Chandra Bose: The Forgotten Hero, and Amitabh Bachchan sang it in the 2012 film Kahaani.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song of resolve: if no one answers your call or walks with you, you should still go forward alone. The mood is steady and defiant rather than sad.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Bengali</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Bengali</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">যদি তোর ডাক শুনে কেউ না আসে তবে একলা চলো রে।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">yadi tora ḍāka śune keu nā āse tabe ekalā calo re.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>If no one comes on hearing your call, then walk alone.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">একলা চলো একলা চলো একলা চলো একলা চলো রে॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">ekalā calo ekalā calo ekalā calo ekalā calo re.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Walk alone, walk alone, walk alone, walk alone.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">যদি কেউ কথা না কয়, ওরে ওরে ও অভাগা,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">yadi keu kathā nā kaẏa, ore ore o abhāgā,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>If no one speaks a word, O you, O you, O unlucky one,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">যদি সবাই থাকে মুখ ফিরায়ে সবাই করে ভয়—</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">yadi sabāi thāke mukha phirāẏe sabāi kare bhaẏa—</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>if all keep their faces turned away, if all are afraid—</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">তবে পরান খুলে</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">tabe parāna khule</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>then, opening your heart,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">ও তুই মুখ ফুটে তোর মনের কথা একলা বলো রে॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">o tui mukha phuṭe tora manera kathā ekalā balo re.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>you, speak out and say what is in your mind alone.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">যদি সবাই ফিরে যায়, ওরে ওরে ও অভাগা,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">yadi sabāi phire yāẏa, ore ore o abhāgā,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>If all turn back, O you, O you, O unlucky one,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">যদি গহন পথে যাবার কালে কেউ ফিরে না চায়—</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">yadi gahana pathe yābāra kāle keu phire nā cāẏa—</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>if, as you set out on the deep, wild road, no one looks back—</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">তবে পথের কাঁটা</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">tabe pathera kām̐ṭā</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>then the thorns of the road</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">ও তুই রক্তমাখা চরণতলে একলা দলো রে॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">o tui raktamākhā caraṇatale ekalā dalo re.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>you, under your blood-smeared feet, tread down alone.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">যদি আলো না ধরে, ওরে ওরে ও অভাগা,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">yadi ālo nā dhare, ore ore o abhāgā,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>If the light does not hold [?], O you, O you, O unlucky one,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">যদি ঝড়-বাদলে আঁধার রাতে দুয়ার দেয় ঘরে—</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">yadi jhaṛa-bādale ām̐dhāra rāte duẏāra deẏa ghare—</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>if on a dark night of storm and rain they shut the doors of their houses—</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">তবে বজ্রানলে</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">tabe bajrānale</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>then in the fire of the thunderbolt</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">আপন বুকের পাঁজর জ্বালিয়ে নিয়ে একলা জ্বলো রে॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">āpana bukera pām̐jara jbāliẏe niẏe ekalā jbalo re.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>set alight the ribs of your own breast and burn alone.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Rabindranath Tagore (1905); the Bengali text as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ekla_Chalo_Re" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (ISO 15919).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (5)</summary><ul><li>The romanization is a strict transliteration of the Bengali letters (the inherent vowel written as 'a'): 'ekalā calo' is sung ekla calo.</li><li>'re' at the line ends is an intimate call to the person addressed, not translated.</li><li>line 2.4: 'mukha phuṭe', literally 'with the mouth opening', to speak out.</li><li>line 4.1: 'ālo nā dhare', literally 'does not hold the light': either no one holds up a lamp for you, or the lamp will not catch.</li><li>line 4.3: 'bajrānala', the fire of the thunderbolt (bajra, Sanskrit vajra).</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
